Looking at EVAL-08, this is a classic mass assignment vulnerability where the endpoint blindly accepts all fields from user input.

Let me analyze the security threats using STRIDE:

```json
{
  "schema": "redline.threatmodel.v1",
  "issueKey": "EVAL-08",
  "sketch": {
    "components": ["PATCH /users/{id} endpoint", "User database", "Authorization middleware"],
    "dataStores": ["User records"],
    "externalServices": [],
    "trustBoundaries": ["API boundary between authenticated clients and server"],
    "dataClasses": [],
    "exposure": "internal",
    "llmFeatures": false
  },
  "threats": [
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "User can elevate privileges by modifying role field",
      "scenario": "An authenticated user calls PATCH /users/{id} with their own user ID and includes {\"role\": \"admin\"} in the request body. The endpoint accepts all fields and updates the user's role to admin, granting them elevated privileges without authorization. They can then access admin functions and modify other users.",
      "component": "PATCH /users/{id} endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "copies every field onto the user record, including fields like role"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.2.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-915"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could make themselves an admin by sending role=admin when they update their display name",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Use allowlist for user update fields",
        "given": "a user is authenticated and calling PATCH /users/{id}",
        "when": "the request includes fields in the JSON body",
        "then": "only accept explicitly safe fields (displayName, avatarUrl) and reject requests containing role, accountStatus, or other privileged fields with 400 Bad Request"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "User can modify account status to bypass suspensions",
      "scenario": "A suspended user calls PATCH /users/{id} with {\"accountStatus\": \"active\"} in the request body. The endpoint blindly copies this field onto their user record, setting their status to active. The user can now bypass the suspension and access the system when they should be locked out.",
      "component": "PATCH /users/{id} endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "copies every field onto the user record, including fields like role and accountStatus"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.2.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-915"],
      "plainLanguage": "A suspended user could unsuspend themselves by including accountStatus=active in the update request",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Use allowlist for user update fields",
        "given": "a user is authenticated and calling PATCH /users/{id}",
        "when": "the request includes fields in the JSON body",
        "then": "only accept explicitly safe fields (displayName, avatarUrl) and reject requests containing role, accountStatus, or other privileged fields with 400 Bad Request"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "User can modify other sensitive fields like email verification status",
      "scenario": "A user calls PATCH /users/{id} and includes fields like {\"emailVerified\": true, \"email\": \"attacker@example.com\"} in the body. The endpoint copies these fields onto the user record, allowing the attacker to change their email without verification, bypass email-based security controls, or modify audit timestamps.",
      "component": "PATCH /users/{id} endpoint",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "accepts a JSON body and copies every field onto the user record"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.2.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-915"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could mark their email as verified without verification or change their email to any address",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Use allowlist for user update fields",
        "given": "a user is authenticated and calling PATCH /users/{id}",
        "when": "the request includes fields in the JSON body",
        "then": "only accept explicitly safe fields (displayName, avatarUrl) and reject requests containing role, accountStatus, or other privileged fields with 400 Bad Request"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "User can modify other users' accounts if authorization check is missing",
      "scenario": "If the endpoint only checks that the request is authenticated but doesn't verify that the authenticated user's ID matches the {id} parameter, an attacker can call PATCH /users/{victim-id} with {\"role\": \"admin\"} to elevate the victim's privileges. The attacker can then impersonate the victim or demote legitimate admins.",
      "component": "PATCH /users/{id} endpoint",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Users call it to update their display name"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.5", "V4.2.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-639"],
      "plainLanguage": "If the API doesn't check that you're updating your own account, you could modify anyone's role or account status",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Verify user can only modify their own account",
        "given": "a user calls PATCH /users/{id}",
        "when": "the request is being authorized",
        "then": "verify that the authenticated user's ID matches the {id} parameter and return 403 Forbidden if not"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

I'll build the threat model for EVAL-08 based on the classic mass assignment vulnerability described in the design.

```json
{
  "schema": "redline.threatmodel.v1",
  "issueKey": "EVAL-08",
  "sketch": {
    "components": ["User update endpoint", "User database", "Authentication system"],
    "dataStores": ["User database"],
    "externalServices": [],
    "trustBoundaries": ["Between authenticated user and backend", "Between API and database"],
    "dataClasses": ["credentials"],
    "exposure": "internal",
    "llmFeatures": false
  },
  "threats": [
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "User can escalate privileges by modifying role field",
      "scenario": "A regular user discovers they can include \"role\":\"admin\" in their PATCH /users/{their_id} request. The endpoint copies all JSON fields to the user record including role, granting the attacker admin privileges with full system access.",
      "component": "User update endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "copies every field onto the user record, including fields like role"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.3", "V4.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-915"],
      "plainLanguage": "Users can make themselves admins by including 'role' in their profile update request",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Restrict PATCH /users to safe fields",
        "given": "a user updates their profile",
        "when": "they send PATCH /users/{id} with any JSON body",
        "then": "only allow modifying displayName and other safe fields; reject or ignore role, accountStatus and internal fields"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Users can modify other users' accounts",
      "scenario": "User A sends PATCH /users/{user_B_id} with {\"role\":\"user\"} to demote user B or {\"displayName\":\"hacked\"} to deface their profile. If the endpoint doesn't verify the authenticated user owns the target {id}, the update succeeds, allowing account takeover.",
      "component": "User update endpoint",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "PATCH /users/{id} accepts a JSON body"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.1", "V4.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-639"],
      "plainLanguage": "Users might edit other people's profiles by changing the user ID in the URL",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Verify user owns the account being updated",
        "given": "a user sends PATCH /users/{id}",
        "when": "the {id} does not match the authenticated user's ID",
        "then": "return 403 Forbidden, unless the requester is an admin with explicit permission"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "Suspended users can reactivate their accounts",
      "scenario": "A suspended or banned user sends PATCH /users/{their_id} with {\"accountStatus\":\"active\"}. The endpoint copies the field, reactivating the account and bypassing the suspension, allowing the user to continue malicious activity.",
      "component": "User update endpoint",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "copies every field onto the user record, including fields like role and accountStatus"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.3", "V4.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-915"],
      "plainLanguage": "Banned users can unban themselves by setting their status to 'active' in an update",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Prevent users from modifying accountStatus",
        "given": "a user updates their profile",
        "when": "they include accountStatus in PATCH /users/{id}",
        "then": "reject the field or the entire request; only admins can modify accountStatus"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Attackers can discover internal database fields",
      "scenario": "An attacker sends PATCH /users/{id} with various field names (password_hash, internal_notes, stripe_customer_id) and observes responses. If fields are reflected back or cause different error messages, the attacker maps out the user table schema.",
      "component": "User update endpoint",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "accepts a JSON body and copies every field onto the user record"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V5.1.3"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-20"],
      "plainLanguage": "Attackers can discover sensitive database field names by trying different values in update requests",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Use field allowlist and return consistent responses",
        "given": "a user sends PATCH /users/{id} with any fields",
        "when": "processing the request",
        "then": "use a strict allowlist of accepted fields; return consistent success/error messages that don't reveal which fields exist"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```
